    "problem_statement": "read_env() method no longer supports returning the path of the loaded .env file\n\nDescription\n\nI noticed that in the latest version, the `read_env()` method no longer supports the `return_path` parameter. This parameter was previously used to get the path of the loaded .env file instead of a boolean indicating if the file was loaded successfully.\n\nWhen trying to use this parameter, I get a TypeError because the parameter has been removed from the method signature.\n\nHere's a simple reproduction script:\n\n```python\nfrom environs import Env\n\nenv = Env()\n# This used to return the path to the .env file\npath = env.read_env(return_path=True)\nprint(f\"Path to .env file: {path}\")\n```\n\nThis code used to work fine in previous versions, returning the path to the .env file as a string, or None if no file was found. Now it fails because the parameter has been removed."
